  "dom_id": "step-q75589",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?",
  "worked_solution": "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}11=3\\lambda  \\\\ 13=-2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$",
